@event world_stairs_ate_a_shoe
# en: The Stairs Ate a Shoe
title [b34164] = 楼梯吃了一只鞋
# en: {Halfway down the east stair the steps shift, and when they settle you are one shoe lighter. Everyone knows where the stairs put shoes, in a heap under the east wing.|The stairs have taken another shoe. You suspect they like the taste of yours.|Shoe gone again. You've come to think of the heap under the east wing as a kind of shoe library.} {You have somewhere to be.|It's raining, and the castle floors are cold.|{season == "winter": Your sock is soaked through in a minute.|The stairs grind smugly behind you.}}
text [94ce4b] = {东楼梯走到一半，台阶挪了一下，等它们停稳，你少了一只鞋。谁都知道楼梯把鞋放在哪儿：东翼底下的一大堆。|楼梯又吞了一只鞋。你怀疑它们就爱吃你的。|鞋又没了。你已经把东翼底下那堆当成某种鞋子图书馆了。} {你还有地方要去。|下着雨，城堡的地板冰凉。|{season == "winter": 一分钟你的袜子就湿透了。|楼梯在你身后得意地嘎吱作响。}}
# en: A great pile of odd shoes heaped in a dusty alcove beneath a castle staircase, a student in one sock digging through it.
scene [8aaa0e] = 城堡楼梯下一处积灰的凹室里堆着一大堆不成对的鞋，一个只穿着一只袜子的学生在里面翻找。
# en: Go and dig through the heap
opt.left.label [e27aa6] = 去鞋堆里翻
# en: {You dig through three centuries of shoes and find yours under a buckled pair that must be older than the towers. You're filthy, late and oddly triumphant.|You find your shoe, and a slipper with a name sewn into it from fifty years ago. You leave the slipper on top, in case.|You know the heap's layers now, like a geologist. Your shoe is on top, almost politely. You stub your toe on the way out anyway.}
opt.left.say.1 [bfb9eb] = {你翻遍三个世纪的鞋，在一双大概比塔楼还老的搭扣鞋底下找到了你的那只。你满身灰、迟到了，却莫名得意。|你找到了自己的鞋，还有一只五十年前缝着名字的拖鞋。你把拖鞋放在最上面，以防万一。|你如今像个地质学家一样熟悉鞋堆的分层。你的鞋就在最上面，几乎可以说彬彬有礼。出来时你还是踢到了脚趾。}
# en: Hop on, one shoe down
opt.right.label [e47056] = 单脚跳着走
# en: {You hop to your next lecture and spend it with one foot in your bag. You take excellent notes, out of spite.|You go about your day in one shoe and one sock. People stare; you make the most of it by reading on the move.|You wear one shoe all day and read between lectures to keep your mind off the cold foot. A first-year asks if it's a fashion. You say yes.}
opt.right.say.1 [d62ad1] = {你单脚跳去上下一节课，整节课都把一只脚塞在书包里。出于赌气，你的笔记记得好极了。|你穿着一只鞋一只袜子过了一天。人们盯着看；你趁机边走边读书。|你一整天都只穿一只鞋，课间读书，好让自己忘了那只冷脚。一个一年级生问这是不是时尚。你说是。}

@event world_duchess_has_news
# en: The Duchess Has News
title [f8bb00] = 公爵夫人有消息
# en: {The Duchess of Ambry, who refuses to stay in her own frame, has squeezed into a pastoral scene by your corridor and is beckoning to you with her fan.|The Duchess again, this time in a portrait of a stern admiral, who looks resigned. "Darling. News."|The Duchess has taken to waiting for you in whatever frame is nearest your door. Today she's sharing a still life with a pear.} {A painted cow is chewing at her elbow.|Her wig is slightly askew from travel.|{season == "winter": She's wearing a painted fur borrowed from a neighbouring frame.|She's fanning herself vigorously.}}
text [d4ab17] = {不肯待在自己画框里的安布里公爵夫人挤进了你走廊边的一幅田园画，正用扇子朝你招手。|又是公爵夫人，这回在一幅严肃海军上将的肖像里，上将一脸认命。“亲爱的。有消息。”|公爵夫人如今总在离你门口最近的画框里等你。今天她和一只梨共享一幅静物画。} {一头画里的奶牛在她手肘边嚼着草。|她的假发因一路奔波有点歪。|{season == "winter": 她披着从隔壁画框借来的一件画中毛皮。|她使劲扇着扇子。}}
# en: An ornate gilt frame in a castle corridor where a powdered portrait duchess has squeezed in beside a placid painted cow, leaning out to whisper.
scene [54301c] = 城堡走廊里一只华丽的镀金画框中，一位扑了粉的肖像公爵夫人挤在一头安详的画中奶牛旁边，探出身来说悄悄话。
# en: Listen to her gossip
opt.left.label [f91911] = 听她讲八卦
# en: {She tells you which professor has a secret allergy to cats and which prefect cries at the opera. You'll never look at either the same way.|She whispers who is courting whom on the third floor, in scandalous detail. You feel like a spy and enjoy it more than you should.|She hands you the castle's secrets like sweets. Some of them are about people you like. You take them anyway.}
opt.left.say.1 [1013a0] = {她告诉你哪位教授暗地里对猫过敏，哪位级长听歌剧会哭。你再也没法用原来的眼光看他们俩了。|她低声讲三楼谁在追求谁，细节耸人听闻。你觉得自己像个间谍，而且乐在其中，有点过了头。|她像发糖一样把城堡的秘密递给你。有些是关于你喜欢的人的。你还是收下了。}
# en: Offer her some news instead
opt.right.label [1d00db] = 反过来给她点消息
# en: {You tell her about your week, a little improved. By supper every portrait in the castle knows a much grander version, and so does half your house, not all of it flattering.|You give her a harmless story about yourself. It comes back to you three days later as a romance, a duel and a minor tragedy, and people keep asking which bit is true.|You feed her an anecdote; she gasps in all the right places. By morning it's a legend with you in it, not entirely flatteringly.}
opt.right.say.1 [9bb9b1] = {你把这周的事稍加润色讲给她听。到晚饭时城堡里每幅肖像都知道了一个宏大得多的版本，你们学院一半的人也知道了，并不全是好话。|你给了她一个关于自己的无害小故事。三天后它回到你耳边，成了一段罗曼史、一场决斗和一出小悲剧，人们不停地问你哪段是真的。|你喂给她一则轶事；她在每个该惊呼的地方惊呼。到第二天早上它已成了传说，你在里面，形象不怎么光彩。}

@event world_laundry_day
# en: Laundry Day
title [d89180] = 洗衣日
# en: {The castle laundry has charmed tubs that wash by themselves, mostly. Your robes are due.|Laundry day. Last month the tubs turned a whole corridor's robes a gentle pink, and nobody has let the tubs forget it.|The laundry. You know which tub shrinks things and which one sings while it spins.} {Someone's socks are trying to escape over the edge.|The steam is so thick you can barely see the lines.|{season == "winter": Everything on the lines is frozen stiff as boards.|The windows are open, and the shirts are trying to fly.}}
text [413f7d] = {城堡洗衣房有施了咒的洗衣桶，会自己洗，大致上。你的长袍该洗了。|洗衣日。上个月洗衣桶把整条走廊的长袍都洗成了淡粉色，至今没人让洗衣桶忘掉这事。|洗衣房。你知道哪个桶会缩水，哪个桶甩干时会唱歌。} {有人的袜子正想翻过桶沿逃走。|蒸汽浓得几乎看不见晾衣绳。|{season == "winter": 绳上的衣服全冻得硬邦邦像木板。|窗户开着，衬衫们正想飞走。}}
# en: Enchanted wash-tubs churning by themselves in a steamy castle laundry, clothes flapping on lines overhead, a student holding up a robe that has turned an alarming colour.
scene [e2a1d3] = 热气腾腾的城堡洗衣房里，施了咒的洗衣桶自己翻搅着，头顶晾衣绳上的衣服随风扑打，一个学生举着一件变了吓人颜色的长袍。
# en: Charm the tub yourself
opt.left.label [a03b19] = 自己给桶施咒
# en: {You coax the tub into a perfect wash. Your robes come out clean, warm and smelling faintly of thunderstorms.|Your robes come out their own colour, which, after the pink month, feels like a victory parade.|You charm the singing tub, and it sings your favourite song while it spins. Everything comes out spotless.}
opt.left.say.1 [1004fa] = {你哄洗衣桶洗得完美。长袍出来干净、温暖，隐约带着雷雨的气息。|你的长袍出来时是它原本的颜色，经历过那个粉色的月份，这感觉像一场凯旋游行。|你给会唱歌的桶施了咒，它甩干时唱着你最喜欢的歌。每件都洗得一尘不染。}
# en: {Your robes come out three sizes smaller. You buy a cheap replacement from the village tailor.|Your robes come out pink, like everyone's last month. The tubs have a sense of humour. You pay for a dye to put it right.|The tub spins so hard your robes leave by the window. You find most of them. The rest cost you a crown at the tailor's.}
opt.left.say.2 [96e530] = {你的长袍出来小了三号。你在镇上裁缝那儿买了件便宜的替换。|你的长袍出来成了粉色，就像上个月大家的那样。洗衣桶很有幽默感。你花钱买了染料补救。|洗衣桶甩得太猛，你的长袍从窗户飞了出去。大部分你找回来了。剩下的在裁缝那儿花了你一个克朗。}
# en: Wash them by hand
opt.right.label [9fc6da] = 用手洗
# en: {You scrub everything at the sink till your knuckles are raw. Clean, at least, and still the right size.|You wash by hand, wring by hand, hang by hand. Your arms ache; your robes stay black.|You wash by hand again. It's slow and cold, and you've come to find it soothing, in a chapped sort of way.}
opt.right.say.1 [d2d39e] = {你在水槽边把所有衣服搓到指节发红。至少干净了，尺码也没变。|手洗、手拧、手晾。你的胳膊酸了；你的长袍还是黑的。|你又用手洗了。又慢又冷，你却渐渐觉得它能让人平静，以一种皲裂的方式。}

@event world_bridge_in_a_gale
# en: The Bridge in a Gale
title [2dae26] = 狂风中的桥
# en: {The wind is up, and the bridge of light between the towers is swaying like a hammock. The long way round is ten minutes and four staircases.|Gale day. The bridges of light sway and ring faintly, like glass in a cupboard.|The bridge in the wind. You know its rhythm now: the sway, the pause, the shudder.} {Two first-years are halfway across, frozen.|A prefect stands at the end shouting safety rules nobody can hear.|{season == "winter": Sleet is coming sideways off the lake.|Leaves are blowing straight through the bridge, as if it isn't there.}}
text [4997b6] = {起风了，塔楼之间的光之桥像吊床一样摇晃。绕远路要十分钟，外加四段楼梯。|大风天。光之桥摇晃着，发出轻微的叮当声，像橱柜里的玻璃杯。|风中的桥。你如今熟悉它的节奏：摇、停、颤。} {两个一年级生走到一半，僵住了。|一位级长站在桥头大喊没人听得见的安全守则。|{season == "winter": 冻雨从湖面横着扫过来。|落叶径直穿过桥身，仿佛桥不存在。}}
# en: A shimmering bridge of light slung between two castle towers, swaying in a howling wind, a student gripping the rail halfway across.
scene [cafd26] = 一座闪烁的光之桥悬在两座城堡塔楼之间，在呼啸的大风中摇摆，一个学生走到桥中间紧握栏杆。
# en: Cross it anyway
opt.left.label [513eed] = 照样过桥
# en: {You walk the swaying bridge like a sailor on a deck and step off the far side with your heart singing.|You cross in the pauses between gusts. The bridge hums under your feet like something alive.|You cross on the rhythm, sway, pause, shudder, step, and arrive before anyone else. The wind claps you on the back.}
opt.left.say.1 [87d9ab] = {你像水手走甲板一样走过摇晃的桥，踏上对岸时心在歌唱。|你趁阵风间隙过桥。桥在你脚下嗡嗡作响，像个活物。|你踩着节奏过桥，摇、停、颤、迈步，比谁都先到。风拍了拍你的背。}
# en: {A gust drops you to your knees halfway across. You crawl the rest, which nobody who saw will ever let you forget.|You freeze in the middle like the first-years and stand there for a long, windy minute. You make it, bruised and shaken.|You misjudge the shudder and the rail catches you in the ribs. You arrive, eventually, aching.}
opt.left.say.2 [fa9547] = {走到一半，一阵狂风把你吹得跪倒。剩下的路你是爬过去的，看见的人永远不会让你忘了这事。|你像那两个一年级生一样僵在中间，在风里站了漫长的一分钟。你过去了，带着淤青，惊魂未定。|你没算准那一颤，栏杆撞上了你的肋骨。你最终到了，浑身酸痛。}
# en: Take the long way round
opt.right.label [d7b80a] = 绕远路
# en: {You take the four staircases and read on the way. You arrive late and better informed.|You take the long way and find a corridor you'd never noticed, with a portrait that winks. You arrive late and curious.|The long way round has become a habit on windy days. You've read most of a book on those four staircases.}
opt.right.say.1 [17d144] = {你走那四段楼梯，一路看书。你迟到了，懂得更多了。|你绕远路，发现了一条从没注意过的走廊，里面有幅会眨眼的肖像。你迟到了，充满好奇。|大风天绕远路已成了你的习惯。在那四段楼梯上，你几乎读完了一整本书。}

@event world_common_room_kettle
# en: The Common-Room Kettle
title [550546] = 公共休息室的水壶
# en: {Your house common room has one kettle, older than anyone in the room, and whoever makes the tea hears everything.|The common-room kettle is whistling. Somebody shouts "Whoever's nearest!" and it's you.|The kettle. You know its temper now: two taps on the lid and it behaves.} {The fire is roaring and people are in their dressing gowns.|Someone has a letter from home and is reading it aloud.|{season == "winter": Snow has drifted against the windows.|The windows are open to the warm night.}}
text [f442c1] = {你们学院的公共休息室只有一把水壶，比屋里任何人都老，谁沏茶谁就什么都听得见。|公共休息室的水壶在叫。有人喊“谁离得近谁去！”，是你。|那把水壶。你如今摸透了它的脾气：敲两下壶盖，它就老实了。} {炉火正旺，大家都穿着睡袍。|有人收到家信，正大声念出来。|{season == "winter": 雪堆到了窗前。|窗户朝温暖的夜敞着。}}
# en: A crowded house common room late in the evening, students in dressing gowns round a fire, a battered kettle starting to whistle on the hob.
scene [19ca1e] = 傍晚时分拥挤的学院公共休息室，穿睡袍的学生们围着炉火，一把破旧的水壶在炉上开始鸣叫。
# en: Make tea for everyone
opt.left.label [a0aa5b] = 给大家沏茶
# en: {You make a pot for twelve with the last of your good leaves. In return you hear who's courting whom, who's failing what, and one secret you'll keep.|You make the tea from your own tin, and everyone moves up to make room for you by the fire. It's the warmest you've been all week.|You make it the way the room likes it now, with the good leaves you bought. Someone says "Thank you" exactly the way your family says it, and your throat goes tight.}
opt.left.say.1 [362f9c] = {你用最后一点好茶叶沏了一壶十二人份的茶。作为回报，你听到了谁在追谁、谁哪门课要挂，还有一个你会守住的秘密。|你用自己罐里的茶叶沏好茶，大家挪了挪，给你在炉边让出位置。这是你一周来最暖和的时候。|你如今照着这屋人喜欢的方式沏，用的是你买的好茶叶。有人说“谢谢”的语气和你家里人一模一样，你的喉咙一紧。}
# en: Take your cup up to bed
opt.right.label [e9fbac] = 端着杯子上床
# en: {You take your tea up to bed and fall asleep before it's cold. The common room laughs below you half the night.|You slip away early with your cup. You miss the best story of the term, you hear in the morning.|You go up early, as you've started to. The noise from below is a nice thing to fall asleep to.}
opt.right.say.1 [01a50c] = {你端着茶上床，茶还没凉就睡着了。公共休息室在你楼下笑了大半夜。|你早早端着杯子溜了。第二天早上你听说错过了本学期最精彩的故事。|你早早上楼，这已成了习惯。楼下的喧闹是很好的催眠曲。}

@event world_the_mirror_asks
# en: The Mirror Asks Again
title [84bb1b] = 镜子又问了
# en: {Once, on your first day, the Sorting Mirror asked who you were before you came. Today, as you cross the entrance hall, it ripples and asks the follow-up, in its voice like cold water. "And now?"|The Sorting Mirror asks its follow-up again: "And now?" It seems to expect a different answer each time.|The Mirror catches you mid-stride. "And now?" You've answered it so often that the question has started to sound like a greeting.} {year == 1: In it, you look younger than you feel.|{final_year: In it, you look like someone about to leave.|In it, your reflection waits for an answer too.}}
text [f13209] = {入学第一天，分院镜问过你来这里之前是谁。今天你穿过门厅时，它泛起涟漪，用冷水般的声音问出了下一个问题：“那么现在呢？”|分院镜又问了它的追问：“那么现在呢？”它似乎每次都期待一个不同的答案。|镜子在你迈步途中叫住了你。“那么现在呢？”你回答过太多次，这个问题听起来已经像一声问候。} {year == 1: 镜中的你看起来比你感觉的年轻。|{final_year: 镜中的你看起来像一个即将离开的人。|镜中的倒影也在等你回答。}}
# en: A tall enchanted mirror in a stone entrance hall, its surface rippling like cold water, a student pausing before it with a bag on one shoulder.
scene [729b6d] = 石砌门厅里一面高大的魔镜，镜面像冷水一样泛着涟漪，一个单肩背包的学生在镜前停下脚步。
# en: Answer it honestly
opt.left.label [76032c] = 诚实地回答
# en: {You tell it plainly what's changed since your first day. The surface stills, and you walk on lighter, as if you'd set something down.|You give it a true answer, with one new thing in it that wasn't there on your first day. The Mirror seems to notice the new thing.|You answer without stopping, plainly, the way you'd answer a friend. It steadies you.}
opt.left.say.1 [6fe304] = {你朴实地告诉它，从入学第一天到现在有什么变了。镜面平静下来，你走开时轻松了，像放下了什么东西。|你给它一个真实的回答，里面有一件入学第一天还没有的新事。镜子似乎注意到了那件新事。|你脚步不停地回答，朴朴实实，就像回答一个朋友。这让你踏实。}
# en: Tell it a pretty lie
opt.right.label [1dd6a6] = 编个漂亮的谎
# en: {You tell it you're secretly a prince in exile. The Mirror ripples, says nothing, and the silence follows you down the corridor.|You tell it something grander than the truth. Your reflection, you'd swear, rolls its eyes.|You lie to it, easily. It doesn't argue. It never argues. That's the worst part.}
opt.right.say.1 [b63b81] = {你告诉它你其实是一位流亡的王子。镜子泛起涟漪，什么也没说，那份沉默一路跟着你走过走廊。|你告诉它一个比真相更宏大的版本。你敢发誓，你的倒影翻了个白眼。|你对它撒了谎，轻而易举。它不争辩。它从不争辩。这才是最糟的。}

@event world_something_in_your_bag
# en: A Stowaway in Your Satchel
title [cfe073] = 书包里的偷渡客
# en: {~A hedge-sprite has moved into your bag and is lining it with your notes.|A small cave newt, glowing faintly orange, is asleep in your inkwell.|A baby owl, still mostly fluff, has climbed into your hood and refuses to leave.} {~Unregistered creatures are tolerated, so long as nobody notices them.|It looks at you as though this was your idea.|It has clearly been there since breakfast.}
text [427902] = {~一只树篱精灵搬进了你的书包，正用你的笔记铺窝。|一只泛着淡橙光的小洞穴蝾螈在你的墨水瓶里睡着了。|一只还几乎全是绒毛的小猫头鹰爬进了你的兜帽，赖着不走。} {~未登记的生灵是被容忍的，只要没人注意到。|它看着你，好像这都是你的主意。|它显然从早饭时就在那儿了。}
# en: A student opening a satchel to find a small magical creature curled up among the books, blinking up at them.
scene [986d38] = 一个学生打开书包，发现一只小小的魔法生灵蜷在书本中间，正眨巴着眼睛抬头看。
# en: Keep it quiet a while
opt.left.label [8f339b] = 悄悄留它一阵
# en: {~You feed it crumbs and hide it from the prefects all day. By evening it's built a nest out of your essay, and you don't mind.|You keep it warm in your pocket all day. Nobody notices; it glows when you laugh.|You carry it about in your hood all day. It hoots once in the middle of a lecture, and you cough to cover it, heroically.}
opt.left.say.1 [46340c] = {~你喂它面包屑，一整天都把它藏着不让级长发现。到傍晚它用你的论文搭了个窝，你也不在意。|你一整天把它揣在口袋里暖着。没人发现；你一笑它就发光。|你一整天把它放在兜帽里带着。它在课上叫了一声，你英勇地咳嗽掩护。}
# en: Take it to the stables
opt.right.label [262d9f] = 送它去马厩
# en: {~You walk it down to the stables. Professor Brask tells it, very kindly, where hedge-sprites belong, and tells you, less kindly, to check your bag.|Professor Brask takes the newt in her palm and tells you three things about cave newts you'll never forget. You miss it a bit on the walk back.|Professor Brask tucks the owlet into a nest box and tells you where its mother is likely to be. You find yourself checking your hood all week.}
opt.right.say.1 [f955d3] = {~你把它送到马厩。布拉斯克教授非常温柔地告诉它树篱精灵该待在哪儿，又不那么温柔地叫你检查书包。|布拉斯克教授把蝾螈托在掌心，告诉你三件你永远忘不了的洞穴蝾螈知识。回来的路上你有点想它。|布拉斯克教授把小猫头鹰塞进一个巢箱，告诉你它妈妈大概在哪儿。你一整周都忍不住摸自己的兜帽。}

@event world_study_group
# en: The Study Group
title [e635da] = 学习小组
# en: {{friend} has a study group going in an empty classroom and a spare seat. "We're doing the hard chapter. Bring your brain, and biscuits."|{friend}'s study group is still going, and still short of biscuits. The chalkboard is a battlefield.|{friend} waves you into the study group. Someone has started a list of who brings what; you're down for biscuits.} {Someone is drawing diagrams in the air with a glowing wand-tip.|Two of them are arguing about a footnote.|{season == "winter": It's freezing; everyone's in scarves.|The window's open, and moths are attending too.}}
text [22a3b8] = {{friend}在一间空教室办了个学习小组，还空着一个座位。“我们在啃最难的那章。带上脑子，还有饼干。”|{friend}的学习小组还在继续，饼干还是不够。黑板成了战场。|{friend}招手叫你进学习小组。有人列了张谁带什么的清单；你负责饼干。} {有人用发光的魔杖尖在空中画示意图。|其中两个人在为一条脚注争论。|{season == "winter": 冷得要命；人人都裹着围巾。|窗开着，飞蛾也来旁听了。}}
# en: A huddle of students round a table piled with books and cold mugs, one drawing diagrams in the air with a glowing wand-tip, late at night.
scene [af6bf6] = 深夜里一群学生挤在堆满书和冷掉马克杯的桌旁，其中一个用发光的魔杖尖在空中画示意图。
# en: Join and work late
opt.left.label [8e18df] = 加入，熬夜学
# en: {You work through the hard chapter together until midnight. It makes sense in the end, mostly because {friend} drew it as a sandwich. You're shattered.|You argue the footnote to a draw and finish the chapter. {friend} walks you back across the bridges, yawning, long past midnight.|You bring the biscuits and an answer. {friend} says you're the group's secret weapon. You go to bed far too late.}
opt.left.say.1 [dc2da6] = {你们一起啃那章啃到半夜。最后终于懂了，主要是因为{friend}把它画成了一个三明治。你累垮了。|你们把脚注争成了平局，也啃完了那章。早过了午夜，{friend}打着哈欠陪你走过光之桥回去。|你带来了饼干和一个答案。{friend}说你是小组的秘密武器。你睡得太晚了。}
# en: Go to bed instead
opt.right.label [fa0361] = 还是去睡觉
# en: {You go to bed early. {friend} tells you at breakfast how good the group was, at length.|You turn in. Through the wall you hear the study group laughing until one, and sleep anyway.|You skip it. {friend} saves you a biscuit, which is somehow worse than a reproach.}
opt.right.say.1 [b7680f] = {你早早睡了。早饭时{friend}跟你讲小组有多好，讲了很久。|你上床了。隔着墙你听见学习小组笑到一点，照样睡着了。|你没去。{friend}给你留了块饼干，这不知怎的比责备还难受。}

@event world_weather_following_you
# en: Weather Following You
title [d1238f] = 跟着你的天气
# en: {~Somebody's weather charm has escaped and attached itself to you. A small rain cloud is following you at head height, drizzling.|A runaway weather charm has found you. A tiny snow flurry is falling on you, and only you.|An escaped weather charm has chosen you. A single warm sunbeam follows you everywhere, even indoors.} {~People step politely out of your way.|A first-year is following you around to see what happens.|It seems pleased with itself.}
text [c93a52] = {~有人的天气咒跑出来，缠上了你。一小朵雨云在你头顶跟着你，淅淅沥沥。|一个失控的天气咒找上了你。一小阵雪只落在你身上。|一个逃逸的天气咒选中了你。一道温暖的阳光走到哪儿跟到哪儿，连室内也不例外。} {~人们客气地给你让路。|一个一年级生跟着你到处走，想看会发生什么。|它似乎很得意。}
# en: A student walking down a castle corridor with a small personal weather charm hovering over their head while everyone else keeps clear.
scene [2d43c6] = 一个学生走在城堡走廊上，头顶悬着一团只属于自己的小小天气咒，其他人都躲得远远的。
# en: Try to dispel it
opt.left.label [5c89d9] = 试着驱散它
# en: {~You unpick the charm thread by thread and the cloud pops like a soap bubble, leaving a small rainbow.|You dispel it; the last snowflake lands on your nose like a goodbye.|You unpick the sunbeam's charm and it fades, regretfully. You feel the cold at once.}
opt.left.say.1 [f01080] = {~你一丝一丝拆开咒语，雨云像肥皂泡一样破了，留下一道小彩虹。|你驱散了它；最后一片雪花落在你鼻尖上，像一声道别。|你拆开阳光的咒语，它依依不舍地淡去了。你立刻觉得冷了。}
# en: {~Your spell makes it a thundercloud. It follows you for the rest of the day, rumbling at anyone who looks at you, and soaks you to the skin.|Your spell doubles it. You spend the day in a small private blizzard and catch a chill.|Your spell turns the sunbeam into a heatwave. You spend the afternoon sweating in a cold castle, red as a lobster.}
opt.left.say.2 [3a6858] = {~你的咒语把它变成了雷雨云。它一整天跟着你，对每个看你的人轰隆作响，还把你浇了个透。|你的咒语让它翻了倍。你在一场私人小暴雪里过了一天，着了凉。|你的咒语把阳光变成了热浪。你在冰冷的城堡里汗流浃背地过了一下午，红得像只龙虾。}
# en: Let it be, and enjoy it
opt.right.label [63b153] = 随它去，享受吧
# en: {~You go about your day in your own private drizzle. By evening it's a castle joke, and you're in on it.|You let it snow on you all day and catch flakes on your tongue in lectures. People talk about it.|You go about your day sunlit and warm. People keep finding reasons to stand next to you.}
opt.right.say.1 [01d68a] = {~你顶着自己的私人细雨过了一天。到傍晚这成了城堡笑话，而你也乐在其中。|你让雪在身上下了一整天，课上还伸舌头接雪花。大家都在议论。|你阳光普照、暖洋洋地过了一天。人们总找借口站到你旁边。}

@event world_lamplighters_round
# en: The Lamplighter's Round
title [8bc53b] = 点灯人的巡夜
# en: {At dusk the old porter makes his round with a long brass pole, lighting the corridor lamps one by one. He's slow these days, and the west corridor is long.|The lamplighter again, at dusk. He nods at the spare pole leaning by the door, as if it had been left there for someone.|The lamplighter's round. The spare pole has a notch carved in it now, about where your hand goes.} {He hums an old song as he goes.|His knees click on every stair.|{season == "winter": It's been dark for an hour already, and every lamp matters.|The evening is long and gold, and he's in no hurry.}}
text [74dd97] = {黄昏时，老门房扛着一根长长的黄铜长杆巡游，把走廊的灯一盏盏点亮。他如今走得慢了，西走廊又很长。|又是黄昏的点灯人。他朝门边靠着的那根备用长杆点点头，好像是特意给谁留的。|点灯人的巡夜。备用长杆上如今刻了个凹口，正好在你手握的位置。} {他边走边哼一支老歌。|他每上一级台阶膝盖都咔哒一响。|{season == "winter": 天已经黑了一个钟头，每一盏灯都很要紧。|傍晚漫长而金黄，他一点也不着急。}}
# en: An old castle porter with a long brass lighting pole lighting the corridor lamps one by one at dusk, a student walking beside him carrying a second pole.
scene [89a471] = 黄昏时分，一位扛着黄铜长杆的老城堡门房把走廊的灯一盏盏点亮，一个学生扛着第二根长杆走在他身旁。
# en: Light the west corridor for him
opt.left.label [d64dc6] = 替他点亮西走廊
# en: {You light them with a flick of fire, one after another, all the way to the end, and feel each one in your fingertips. The porter says the corridor has never looked so cheerful, and he's been doing it forty years.|You take the far end with the spare pole and a little flame. He takes the near end and tells you about the castle when he was a boy. You're drained, and lit up.|You light the west corridor in a single walk now, a flame at every lamp, and you're light-headed by the end. The porter lights his pipe off the last one.}
opt.left.say.1 [569737] = {你弹指生火，一盏接一盏一直点到尽头，每一盏都在你指尖留下一丝灼意。门房说走廊从没这么喜气过，而他干这活四十年了。|你拿着备用长杆和一小簇火负责远端。他负责近端，给你讲他小时候的城堡。你被掏空了，也被点亮了。|如今你一趟就能点亮整条西走廊，每盏灯一簇火，走到头时你有些头晕。门房就着最后一盏点着了烟斗。}
# en: Walk with him and listen
opt.right.label [5ab4ae] = 陪他走，听他讲
# en: {He tells you which lamps never stay lit and why, and which ghost likes the dark. You walk the whole round and forget you had anywhere to be.|He tells you about a headmistress, long ago, who lit every lamp in the castle with one snap of her fingers. "Showing off," he says fondly.|He tells you a story for every lamp. You've heard some before. You don't mind.}
opt.right.say.1 [ae9f40] = {他告诉你哪些灯总也点不亮、为什么，哪个鬼魂喜欢黑暗。你陪他走完全程，忘了自己还有地方要去。|他讲起很久以前有位院长，打一个响指就点亮了城堡里所有的灯。“爱显摆。”他慈爱地说。|他每盏灯讲一个故事。有些你听过了。你不介意。}

@event world_the_warm_flue
# en: The Warm Flue
title [8e17cb] = 暖烟道
# en: {Behind the kitchens there's a window niche where the chimney flue runs warm through the stone. It's the best nap in the castle, and it's free.|The warm niche by the flue is free again. Someone has left a cushion, and a note: "FIRST COME".|The niche by the flue. You've learned when the kitchens fire the ovens, and when it's warmest.} {The corridor is quiet.|A cat is already asleep in one corner.|{season == "winter": Frost is on the window; the stone is toasty.|The window is open, and a breeze plays over it.}}
text [edf433] = {厨房后面有个窗龛，烟道从石墙里穿过，暖烘烘的。那是城堡里最好的午睡处，还不要钱。|烟道边的暖窗龛又空着。有人留了个坐垫，还有张字条：“先到先得”。|烟道边的窗龛。你摸清了厨房什么时候烧烤炉，什么时候最暖。} {走廊里很安静。|一只猫已经睡在一个角落里了。|{season == "winter": 窗上结着霜；石头却暖烘烘的。|窗开着，微风拂过。}}
# en: A sunny stone window niche in a castle corridor beside a warm chimney flue, a student curled up asleep in it with a book over their face.
scene [6f162f] = 城堡走廊里一处洒满阳光的石砌窗龛，紧挨着温暖的烟道，一个学生蜷在里面睡着了，脸上盖着一本书。
# en: Curl up for an hour
opt.left.label [71f6b4] = 蜷起来睡一小时
# en: {You sleep like a loaf in an oven. You wake to find you've missed the reading you meant to do, and don't regret it as much as you should.|You nap on the cushion and wake an hour later, rosy and behind on everything.|You nap through the oven-firing, the warmest hour. Your notes stay shut; your soul is restored.}
opt.left.say.1 [9331e4] = {你睡得像炉子里的面包。醒来发现错过了本打算读的书，却没有应有的那么后悔。|你枕着坐垫打了个盹，一小时后醒来，脸色红润，什么都落下了。|你一觉睡过了烧炉的时辰，最暖的那一小时。笔记一页没翻；灵魂得到了修复。}
# en: Read in it instead
opt.right.label [97e203] = 改在里面看书
# en: {You read in the warm with your back to the flue and finish a whole chapter without once nodding off. Nearly.|You read with the cushion behind you and the warmth seeping in. It's the most comfortable studying you've ever done.|You read through the warmest hour and manage not to sleep. It's an act of will, and a pleasant one.}
opt.right.say.1 [7ffb2e] = {你背靠烟道在暖意里看书，读完一整章，一次也没打瞌睡。差一点。|你倚着坐垫看书，暖意慢慢渗进来。这是你做过最舒服的功课。|你熬过最暖的那一小时愣是没睡着。这需要意志力，也是一种享受。}

@event world_margin_argument
# en: The Argument in the Margins
title [da21ee] = 页边的争论
# en: {Your second-hand textbook came with an argument in the margins. Two former owners, decades apart, have been disagreeing about chapter four in ink.|You've reached chapter six, and the margin argument has followed. A third hand has joined in, in green, mostly to tell them both to calm down.|The margins again. You know the three hands now: the furious one, the patient one, the green one who draws small owls.} {Today it's about whether a spell can be "polite".|Today one of them has written "SEE ME AFTER" to the other, forty years late.|{final_year: You wonder who'll own this book after you.|Someone has pressed a flower at the page where they finally agree.}}
text [d88df4] = {你的二手课本附赠了一场页边争论。两位相隔几十年的前主人，一直在用墨水争论第四章。|你读到第六章了，页边的争论一路跟了过来。第三种笔迹加入进来，用绿墨水，主要是叫那两位都冷静点。|又是页边。你如今认得三种笔迹了：暴躁的那位、耐心的那位，还有画小猫头鹰的绿墨水那位。} {今天争的是一个咒语能不能算“有礼貌”。|今天其中一位给另一位写了句“下课来找我”，迟了四十年。|{final_year: 你不禁想，你之后这本书会归谁。|在他们终于达成一致的那一页，有人夹了一朵压花。}}
# en: An open second-hand textbook covered in handwritten margin notes in three different inks, arrows and crossings-out running between them, a student's pencil hovering.
scene [51688a] = 一本摊开的二手课本，页边写满了三种墨色的手写批注，箭头和涂改在其间穿梭，一个学生的铅笔悬在上方。
# en: Add your own note to the argument
opt.left.label [cce37c] = 在争论里添一笔
# en: {You write a careful note siding with the patient one and citing the page. It feels like joining a very old, very slow conversation.|You add a fourth hand, in pencil, and settle the question of polite spells for good. You're sure someone will disagree in thirty years.|You write a small note to the green one, thanking them for the owls. The book feels more yours for it.}
opt.left.say.1 [6ef223] = {你写下一条仔细的批注，站在耐心的那位一边，还注明了页码。感觉像加入了一场非常古老、非常缓慢的对话。|你用铅笔添上第四种笔迹，一劳永逸地解决了礼貌咒语的问题。你确信三十年后会有人不同意。|你给绿墨水那位写了张小纸条，谢谢那些猫头鹰。这本书因此更像你的了。}
# en: Ignore them and read the text
opt.right.label [c6a5d0] = 不理他们，只读正文
# en: {You cover the margins with your hand and read the actual chapter. It's duller than the argument, and you remember more of it.|You read the printed text and resolutely ignore three people shouting in ink. You lose the thread twice anyway.|You read straight through without once looking at the margins, which takes willpower. The chapter goes in; the fun stays out.}
opt.right.say.1 [541b08] = {你用手挡住页边，读真正的章节。比争论无聊，你却记住得更多。|你读印刷的正文，坚决无视三个用墨水吵架的人。你还是走神了两次。|你一眼都没看页边，一口气读完，这需要意志力。章节读进去了；乐趣留在了外面。}

@event world_door_that_wasnt_there
# en: A Door That Wasn't There
title [f3b1d3] = 昨天还没有的门
# en: {~In a corridor you walk every day there is a door that wasn't there yesterday, small and arched, with a brass handle worn by hands that aren't yours.|A door has appeared in the bare wall by the stairs, painted a cheerful blue, with no keyhole.|There's a new door at the end of a corridor that used to just end. Behind it, something is ticking.} {~The castle does this. It always puts them back.|Nobody else seems to notice it.|It's slightly ajar.}
text [e5643c] = {~在你每天都走的一条走廊里，出现了一扇昨天还没有的门，小小的拱门，黄铜把手被不是你的手磨得发亮。|楼梯边光秃秃的墙上出现了一扇门，漆成欢快的蓝色，没有钥匙孔。|一条原本是死胡同的走廊尽头多了一扇新门。门后有东西在滴答作响。} {~城堡常干这种事。它总会把门收回去。|别人似乎都没注意到它。|它虚掩着。}
# en: A small arched wooden door set into a castle corridor wall where there was only bare stone yesterday, its brass handle faintly warm.
scene [9a4be7] = 城堡走廊的墙上嵌着一扇小小的木拱门，昨天那里还只有光秃秃的石头，它的黄铜把手微微温热。
# en: Open it and look inside
opt.left.label [e83222] = 打开往里看
# en: {~Behind it is a tiny library of books about doors, all open at the same page. You read for an hour, and when you come out, it's gone.|Behind it is a cupboard full of preserved pears, labelled in a hand from two centuries ago. You take one. It's delicious, and the door is gone by supper.|Behind it is a room full of clocks, every one of them right. You set your watch by them and leave, and the door seals behind you.}
opt.left.say.1 [73f726] = {~门后是一座小小的图书馆，全是关于门的书，都翻在同一页。你读了一小时，出来时它不见了。|门后是一个装满蜜饯梨的橱柜，标签是两个世纪前的笔迹。你拿了一个。很好吃，门到晚饭时就不见了。|门后是一间满是钟的屋子，每一座都准。你照着它们对好表离开，门在你身后封上了。}
# en: {~Behind it is a spiral stair up to nowhere. Halfway, the steps get narrower than your foot, and you come down with a turned ankle and the door gone.|Behind it is a broom cupboard, and the brooms are not pleased to see you. You leave with bruises.|Behind it is a room full of clocks, all of them striking at once, right in your ears. You stagger out with a headache that lasts till bedtime.}
opt.left.say.2 [68dd49] = {~门后是一道通往虚无的螺旋楼梯。走到一半，台阶窄得放不下你的脚，你崴了脚踝下来，门已经不见了。|门后是一个扫帚间，扫帚们见到你很不高兴。你带着一身淤青离开。|门后是一间满是钟的屋子，所有钟同时在你耳边敲响。你踉踉跄跄地出来，头疼到睡觉。}
# en: Chalk a cross on it, walk on
opt.right.label [d903e5] = 画个叉，继续走
# en: {~You chalk a neat cross on it, like the old marks you've seen on other walls. By tomorrow the door is gone, but your cross is still there.|You mark it and keep walking. Some doors are invitations; some are tests. You don't feel like being tested today.|You leave your mark and a note of the date. You're keeping a map of where the doors appear. The castle, you suspect, is keeping one of you.}
opt.right.say.1 [0929e9] = {~你在门上用粉笔画了个整齐的叉，就像你在别的墙上见过的那些旧记号。第二天门不见了，你的叉还在。|你画好记号继续走。有些门是邀请；有些是考验。你今天不想被考验。|你留下记号和日期。你在绘制一张门出现位置的地图。你怀疑城堡也在绘制一张你的地图。}

@event world_paper_bird
# en: The Paper-Bird Jam
title [daaef2] = 纸鸟大堵塞
# en: {Somebody's routing charm has gone wrong, and every paper bird in your tower is turning in at your window instead of its own. There are eleven on the sill, and more coming.|The paper-bird jam again. Someone has pinned a sign to your door that says SORTING OFFICE.|The birds know your window now. They queue on the sill like regulars at a counter.} {One is pecking at the glass with real urgency.|Two of them are fighting over the latch.|{season == "winter": They're dusted with frost and shivering.|Rain has smudged half the names on their wings.}}
text [6e37b0] = {有人的寻路咒出了岔子，你们塔里每一只纸鸟都拐进了你的窗户，而不是它们自己的。窗台上已经有十一只，还有更多正在飞来。|纸鸟又堵车了。有人在你门上钉了块牌子，写着“分拣处”。|纸鸟们如今认得你的窗户了。它们在窗台上排着队，像柜台前的老主顾。} {有一只正十万火急地啄着玻璃。|有两只在为窗闩打架。|{season == "winter": 它们落满了霜，瑟瑟发抖。|雨水把一半翅膀上的名字洇开了。}}
# en: Dozens of folded paper birds crowding and flapping against a dormitory window, a student trying to catch them in both hands.
scene [3caa48] = 几十只折好的纸鸟挤在宿舍窗户上扑腾，一个学生正试图用双手把它们捧住。
# en: Sort them and send them on
opt.left.label [a8d569] = 分拣好再送走
# en: {You read wings, untangle a knot of six and send every bird to its proper window. An hour gone, and your eyes ache from the handwriting.|You sort them by tower and floor on your bed and launch them in order. It's dull work, and every one of them arrives.|You sort the queue at speed now, like a postmaster. It's still an hour you meant to spend on something else.}
opt.left.say.1 [a025fd] = {你辨认翅膀上的字，解开一团六只缠在一起的，把每只鸟都送往正确的窗口。一小时过去了，那些字迹看得你眼睛发酸。|你在床上按塔楼和楼层分好，依次放飞。活儿很枯燥，每一只都送到了。|如今你分拣得像个邮政局长一样快。可这依然是你本打算花在别处的一小时。}
# en: Open the window and shoo them
opt.right.label [501bea] = 开窗把它们轰走
# en: {You fling the window wide and wave your arms. The birds scatter like startled pigeons, and half of them land at the wrong doors. By supper it's been traced to you.|You shoo them out in a great flapping cloud. It's glorious. Two notes end up in the headmistress's tower, and people know whose window they came from.|You open the window and let the flock go where it likes. Wrong doors all over the tower, and a reputation as the leak.}
opt.right.say.1 [2d6a11] = {你把窗户大开，挥舞双臂。纸鸟像受惊的鸽子一样四散，一半落在了错误的门口。到晚饭时，源头查到了你头上。|你把它们轰成一大片扑腾的云。壮观极了。有两张字条落进了院长塔，大家都知道是从谁的窗户飞出来的。|你打开窗，任鸟群爱去哪去哪。整座塔到处是送错的门，你落了个“漏洞”的名声。}

@event world_practice_next_door
# en: The Practice Next Door
title [3cc08e] = 隔壁的练习
# en: {Someone in the room next door is learning a piece on a charmed fiddle. They play the same eight bars over and over, and the fiddle sighs at every wrong note.|The fiddle next door has got as far as the sixteenth bar. It still sighs, but less.|The piece next door is almost a piece now. You know it as well as the player does, wrong notes included.} {You were trying to read.|It's late, and the wall is thin.|{season == "winter": The radiator carries it into your room like a second instrument.|The windows are open, and half the corridor can hear it.}}
text [bf4ce1] = {隔壁房间有人在用一把施了咒的小提琴练一首曲子。他们翻来覆去拉同样的八小节，每拉错一个音，琴就叹一口气。|隔壁的小提琴已经拉到第十六小节了。还在叹气，但少了些。|隔壁那首曲子差不多像首曲子了。你和拉琴的人一样熟悉它，连错音都熟悉。} {你本来想看书的。|夜深了，墙很薄。|{season == "winter": 暖气片把琴声送进你屋里，像第二件乐器。|窗户开着，半条走廊都听得见。}}
# en: A student at a desk with their head in their hands, a thin wall between them and an unseen musician, music drifting under the door.
scene [458ac3] = 一个学生坐在桌前双手抱头，一堵薄墙另一侧是看不见的乐手，琴声从门缝下飘进来。
# en: Knock and offer to help
opt.left.label [e79c34] = 敲门去帮忙
# en: {You knock. The player is a mortified first-year who has never had a lesson. You hum the eight bars properly until they have them, which is well past midnight.|You knock, and stay to count the beat for an hour. The sixteenth bar comes right for the first time; you both cheer, and the neighbours bang on the wall.|The door's already open when you knock. You tap out the tricky bit on the tabletop while they follow. Bed is very late.}
opt.left.say.1 [18b2d5] = {你敲了门。拉琴的是个窘得要命、从没上过课的一年级生。你把那八小节正确地哼给 TA 听，直到 TA 学会，那时早过了午夜。|你敲门进去，留下来帮着打了一小时拍子。第十六小节头一回拉对了；你们俩一起欢呼，邻居们捶起了墙。|你敲门时门已经开着。你在桌面上用手指敲出难的那段，TA 跟着拉。睡觉时已经很晚了。}
# en: Pillow over your head
opt.right.label [4b2d41] = 用枕头捂住头
# en: {You read with a pillow pressed to one ear. You get through the chapter; the eight bars get into your dreams.|You work through it while the fiddle sighs at the sixteenth bar every four minutes, regular as a clock. You finish your reading through gritted teeth.|You study to the sound of it, grudgingly. Annoyingly, it helps; you'll be humming it in the exam.}
opt.right.say.1 [e42471] = {你把枕头压在一只耳朵上看书。这一章读完了；那八小节钻进了你的梦里。|你埋头苦读，小提琴每隔四分钟就在第十六小节叹一口气，准得像钟。你咬着牙读完了。|你不情不愿地伴着琴声学习。可气的是，这居然有用；考试时你会哼起它来。}
